  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records provide several pieces pointing in the right direction: R12 establishes the exact AlCoCrFeNi2.1 alloy and its eutectic FCC/B2 structure; R5 reports L12 and B2 precipitation after thermomechanical processing in a closely related Al-containing CoCrFeNi alloy; and R17 discusses ordering and phase stability in the same alloy family. R2, R6, R7, R8, and R14 provide related links among chemical order or fault energy, planar dislocation modes, interfaces, stress concentration, and temperature-dependent plasticity. R18 additionally shows that annealing-based processing can create controlled coherent precipitation in a dual-phase HEA. Nevertheless, none of the records describes intermediate-temperature aging of as-cast AlCoCrFeNi2.1, identifies maximal L12 ordering and jagged FCC/B2 boundaries near 600 °C, or predicts the specific ductile-brittle-ductile reversal and its recovery near 750 °C. Combining the records could motivate an aging study and an ordering-controlled deformation hypothesis, but the particular intervention, temperature window, boundary morphology, and non-monotonic mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
